Bedrock — business-strategist angle only: The strategic value proposition is a fully managed, serverless service reaching multiple providers' foundation models (AI21, Anthropic, Cohere, Meta, Mistral, Stability, Amazon Nova) through one unified API — no infrastructure to manage, fast time to value, and the flexibility to switch or compare models without re-architecting. Customisation is private: fine-tuning creates a separate copy of the model in your account, your training data stays in your AWS environment, the base model is unchanged, and nothing is shared back with the model provider — that's the answer to any data-sovereignty or IP-leakage question. Two commercial models: on-demand (pay per input/output token, per input token for embeddings, per image) for variable or experimental workloads, and provisioned throughput (purchase model units, priced on tokens per minute) for large, consistent workloads — and note that provisioned throughput is mandatory for fine-tuning, which is a cost-planning implication, not a technical one. Cost-optimisation talking point: Amazon Nova models are positioned at ~75% cheaper than comparable best-performing models in their intelligence class. Responsible-AI/governance levers to name in a business answer: Guardrails for blocking unwanted content, model evaluation before deployment, and watermark detection for generated images.